WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.392 --> 00:00:02.450
- Things can't go worse than that.

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.622
I'll sleep on the floor,
I'll sleep on the pavement.

00:00:04.622 --> 00:00:06.005
I've never believed in super stardom,

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:07.975
I believe that if people like your work

00:00:07.975 --> 00:00:11.233
which is your performance
in front of the camera,

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:12.710
that is what attracts them to you.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.304
Everyone said that I was
making the biggest mistake

00:00:15.304 --> 00:00:18.313
of my life by going into television.

00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:20.529
If things happen
according to how you wish,

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:23.777
that's good, but if they
don't, that's even better.

00:00:23.777 --> 00:00:26.904
There'll always be somebody
who's going to be younger,

00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:28.358
better than you.

00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:32.426
- He's an Indian actor who's
appeared in over 180 films

00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:35.417
in a career that has
spanned over four decades.

00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:37.843
He's widely regarded as
one of the most influential

00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:40.792
and important figures in
Indian cinema history.

00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:43.264
He was so dominant over
the Indian film market

00:00:43.264 --> 00:00:45.849
in the '70's and '80's
that people called him

00:00:45.849 --> 00:00:47.744
a one-man industry.

00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:48.922
He's Amitabh Bachchan,

00:00:48.922 --> 00:00:51.531
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:53.091
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:53.091 --> 00:00:54.669
and I'm curious to figure out which one

00:00:54.669 --> 00:00:55.885
you guys like the best.

00:00:55.885 --> 00:00:57.059
And as always, if you hear something

00:00:57.059 --> 00:00:59.341
that really is motivating
and inspiring to you,

00:00:59.341 --> 00:01:00.818
please leave it in the comments below

00:01:00.818 --> 00:01:01.784
and put quotes around it

00:01:01.784 --> 00:01:04.154
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:04.154 --> 00:01:06.996
(swoosh)

00:01:06.996 --> 00:01:09.746
(dramatic music)

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:19.531
- I doubt if there's any
actor in the entire world,

00:01:19.531 --> 00:01:22.787
with all due respects to Mr. De Niro,

00:01:22.787 --> 00:01:26.181
when they haven't gone
ahead and done a film

00:01:26.181 --> 00:01:28.681
and it hasn't turned out to be

00:01:31.441 --> 00:01:34.490
the kind of film that
they believed it would be,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.383
or it has been so incredibly bad

00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:40.466
that it's just kind of
forgotten in their vision.

00:01:43.134 --> 00:01:44.634
Sometimes we do films

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:48.272
because we believe in the
person who was going to make it.

00:01:48.272 --> 00:01:50.326
Sometimes it's a gesture.

00:01:50.326 --> 00:01:54.059
Sometimes it's your
own belief going wrong.

00:01:54.059 --> 00:01:55.444
We all make mistakes,

00:01:55.444 --> 00:01:58.581
and if they were mistakes, fine,

00:01:58.581 --> 00:02:02.875
but I will not deride any
film that I have done.

00:02:02.875 --> 00:02:05.452
I will stay with every
film that I have made.

00:02:05.452 --> 00:02:08.689
And I will say that I had
a great time making them

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:11.328
irrespective of what
happened at the box office.

00:02:11.328 --> 00:02:15.712
Failure is a part of
every human being's life.

00:02:15.712 --> 00:02:18.400
To think that failure is never
ever going to come to you

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.358
is the first mistake.

00:02:20.358 --> 00:02:22.052
It is bound to come some point in time,

00:02:22.052 --> 00:02:25.657
even if you're riding
a huge wave of success.

00:02:25.657 --> 00:02:27.015
I think it's important

00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:30.665
that at the back of your
mind you should be weary

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:33.832
that tomorrow everything can go wrong.

00:02:36.257 --> 00:02:40.174
Whenever I think like
this I immediately think,

00:02:41.439 --> 00:02:44.957
"Okay, suppose I was to lose everything.

00:02:44.957 --> 00:02:49.387
"What is the bottom line that
would keep me comfortable?"

00:02:49.387 --> 00:02:52.561
So I go back to where I was born

00:02:52.561 --> 00:02:55.969
and how I was brought home in a tonga

00:02:55.969 --> 00:02:59.508
wrapped in a (mumbles), and I say,

00:02:59.508 --> 00:03:03.545
"The worst is that things
can't go worse than that.

00:03:03.545 --> 00:03:04.461
"I'll sleep on the floor.

00:03:04.461 --> 00:03:07.025
"I'll sleep on the pavement."

00:03:07.025 --> 00:03:08.696
So you look at the bottom line.

00:03:08.696 --> 00:03:11.742
- And that makes you
feel it's not that bad?

00:03:11.742 --> 00:03:12.575
That gives you strength?

00:03:12.575 --> 00:03:14.588
- You feel that if you're
prepared for the worst.

00:03:14.588 --> 00:03:15.421
A lot of people can say,

00:03:15.421 --> 00:03:18.477
"Look, I can't do without
a house with 10 bedrooms."

00:03:18.477 --> 00:03:22.611
Or, "I need two cars, and I need a phone."

00:03:22.611 --> 00:03:24.820
Everyone has different priorities,

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.612
but I think that what's really,

00:03:26.612 --> 00:03:29.756
what I feel is that I think
about the bottom line,

00:03:29.756 --> 00:03:33.479
and when it isn't too
difficult to cope with

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:34.814
I feel very satisfied.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:38.336
From there on you say,
"Okay, this is where I am.

00:03:38.336 --> 00:03:40.316
"I have to move forward from here.

00:03:40.316 --> 00:03:43.511
"This is the worst scenario,
and now I must move forward."

00:03:43.511 --> 00:03:48.030
So every day you keep digging
away, digging at something,

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.438
poking at something,

00:03:49.438 --> 00:03:53.110
and even if it's a centimeter
that you move forward,

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:54.961
at least you move forward.

00:03:54.961 --> 00:03:59.125
I'm always reminded of an
incident of my father's.

00:03:59.125 --> 00:04:02.645
We used to live in Delhi
at William and Crescent,

00:04:02.645 --> 00:04:06.549
which is near (mumbles) house
where (mumbles) used to stay.

00:04:06.549 --> 00:04:08.123
And at that time the diplomatic enclave,

00:04:08.123 --> 00:04:11.126
which is now very, very habitated,

00:04:11.126 --> 00:04:12.816
was just wilderness and wild jungle,

00:04:12.816 --> 00:04:16.060
and he used to go for his
morning walk in that region.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.600
And he used to leave
at 3:40 in the morning

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.788
and come back at 5, 6.

00:04:19.788 --> 00:04:23.955
And he developed a taste for
looking at rocks on the road

00:04:27.789 --> 00:04:32.098
and finding some kind of
an image or a figure in it.

00:04:32.098 --> 00:04:32.931
And he would bring it home

00:04:32.931 --> 00:04:35.357
and with just a stroke of a paintbrush

00:04:35.357 --> 00:04:36.698
bring out the dimension,

00:04:36.698 --> 00:04:38.599
or just leave the stone like that and say,

00:04:38.599 --> 00:04:39.711
"Look, this looks like a bird,"

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:42.865
or, "This looks like a human,"
and he developed this thing.

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:44.504
One day he came back from his walk

00:04:44.504 --> 00:04:47.178
and he woke me up, as he normally would,

00:04:47.178 --> 00:04:48.459
at 6 in the morning and say,

00:04:48.459 --> 00:04:50.273
"Can you just help me?

00:04:50.273 --> 00:04:52.169
"I've got something which I cannot lift,

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:54.100
"so help me bring it in."

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.187
So I went to the gate

00:04:55.187 --> 00:04:58.937
and there was a huge
boulder about this size.

00:05:00.994 --> 00:05:03.549
And I said, "Look, you
can't possibly think

00:05:03.549 --> 00:05:04.598
"that I can move this."

00:05:04.598 --> 00:05:07.584
So we called about 10, 20 people,

00:05:07.584 --> 00:05:10.155
and they pushed this huge boulder

00:05:10.155 --> 00:05:11.507
and brought it into our veranda

00:05:11.507 --> 00:05:14.146
where he wanted it to be put.

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:14.979
That dinner I asked him,

00:05:14.979 --> 00:05:19.891
"Look, this is fine, but how
did this come up to the gate?"

00:05:19.891 --> 00:05:23.902
So he said, "You know, I went for my walk.

00:05:23.902 --> 00:05:25.297
"I saw this huge rock.

00:05:25.297 --> 00:05:27.288
"I saw this shape in it, and I liked it

00:05:27.288 --> 00:05:28.785
"and I wanted to move it."

00:05:28.785 --> 00:05:32.952
So every day I go I move it
one inch and I come back.

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:35.518
In three months it's
landed up at the gate.

00:05:35.518 --> 00:05:37.768
(applause)

00:05:39.596 --> 00:05:41.920
So it's a great example

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.998
that if you just keep
pushing something every day,

00:05:45.998 --> 00:05:47.093
it may look small,

00:05:47.093 --> 00:05:48.431
but I think the important thing

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:50.340
is that you're moving forward.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.835
And there is some amount of satisfaction

00:05:51.835 --> 00:05:52.706
that you've done something

00:05:52.706 --> 00:05:56.077
which is somewhere giving
you some satisfaction,

00:05:56.077 --> 00:05:57.603
and that eventually you will bring

00:05:57.603 --> 00:05:59.792
that huge boulder to its destiny.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.464
I have never believed in superstardom.

00:06:01.464 --> 00:06:03.395
I believe that if people like your work,

00:06:03.395 --> 00:06:06.703
which is your performance
in front of the camera,

00:06:06.703 --> 00:06:08.771
that is what attracts them to you.

00:06:08.771 --> 00:06:10.795
I don't see any other medium being used

00:06:10.795 --> 00:06:12.703
to attract audiences.

00:06:12.703 --> 00:06:16.145
It's eventually your film, and
that's what's going to work.

00:06:16.145 --> 00:06:17.697
You may have the greatest managers

00:06:17.697 --> 00:06:19.673
and the greatest PR machines,

00:06:19.673 --> 00:06:22.313
but unless you perform well
in front of the camera,

00:06:22.313 --> 00:06:23.479
it's of no use.

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:27.646
- For 45 years he has
reflected and shaped society.

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.604
When he got angry, the nation got angry.

00:06:31.604 --> 00:06:35.077
When he got injured, the
nation came to a standstill.

00:06:35.077 --> 00:06:37.631
And now, when the common man of the nation

00:06:37.631 --> 00:06:39.357
has taken to the streets of (mumbles)

00:06:39.357 --> 00:06:40.960
he made (mumbles).

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.210
- What you said was more an instigation

00:06:46.515 --> 00:06:49.015
by the people who write films,

00:06:50.917 --> 00:06:52.144
and if credit has to be given

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:55.556
to the mood of the country at the moment

00:06:55.556 --> 00:06:57.599
and how it is reflected in cinema,

00:06:57.599 --> 00:07:01.599
you will have to credit
the writers of the time.

00:07:04.175 --> 00:07:07.898
So if you felt that there
was a moment in the film

00:07:07.898 --> 00:07:09.504
which talked about anger,

00:07:09.504 --> 00:07:12.741
which talked about going
against the system,

00:07:12.741 --> 00:07:14.429
wanting to fight alone,

00:07:14.429 --> 00:07:18.270
then Salim-Javed who wrote
the script of Zanjeer

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.175
at that point of time
will have to be credited,

00:07:21.175 --> 00:07:24.092
because they were the ones
that thought about it.

00:07:24.092 --> 00:07:26.004
Many a times this question is posed to me,

00:07:26.004 --> 00:07:28.971
but I'm merely another actor

00:07:28.971 --> 00:07:32.253
who has just happened to be
lucky enough to get that role.

00:07:32.253 --> 00:07:34.657
- You're not merely
anything, Mr. Bachchan.

00:07:34.657 --> 00:07:36.518
- Yeah, right now I am.

00:07:36.518 --> 00:07:38.002
There came a turning point

00:07:38.002 --> 00:07:41.085
where mainly due to financial reasons

00:07:42.571 --> 00:07:46.154
I got an offer from
television to host KBC,

00:07:47.383 --> 00:07:49.740
which was the Hindi version

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.206
of Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?

00:07:52.206 --> 00:07:53.873
And I took that job,

00:07:55.722 --> 00:08:00.282
and I felt that that was
a savior in many respects,

00:08:00.282 --> 00:08:03.130
because suddenly it gave me an opportunity

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:06.630
to interact with the people of the nation,

00:08:08.564 --> 00:08:10.216
with the common man,

00:08:10.216 --> 00:08:13.322
with people whom we knew
existed in our country

00:08:13.322 --> 00:08:16.572
but we've never known their conditions.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.218
It was a great eye opener for me

00:08:19.218 --> 00:08:21.593
apart from the fact that
it was a novel experience

00:08:21.593 --> 00:08:23.950
for me to be working on television.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:25.997
Everyone said that I was making

00:08:25.997 --> 00:08:30.252
the biggest mistake of my
life by going into television,

00:08:30.252 --> 00:08:33.171
reducing myself from
a 70 millimeter screen

00:08:33.171 --> 00:08:35.724
onto a 25 inch TV screen

00:08:35.724 --> 00:08:38.699
felt was almost like reducing your stature

00:08:38.699 --> 00:08:41.282
to 25 inches of a TV screen.

00:08:41.282 --> 00:08:42.897
But there were compulsions,

00:08:42.897 --> 00:08:47.064
and I went ahead and I'm
now in the 12th year of KBC.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.501
I keep quoting my father all the time,

00:08:50.501 --> 00:08:54.334
and I'm sure I've done
this millions of times.

00:08:57.245 --> 00:08:58.372
One of the first things he taught me

00:08:58.372 --> 00:09:01.622
was (speaks in Hindi),

00:09:02.562 --> 00:09:05.815
if things happen according
to how you wish, that's good,

00:09:05.815 --> 00:09:08.204
but if they don't, that's even better.

00:09:08.204 --> 00:09:10.432
And I could never understand that.

00:09:10.432 --> 00:09:11.597
And when I asked him he said,

00:09:11.597 --> 00:09:14.049
"If they're not happening
according to your wish,

00:09:14.049 --> 00:09:16.295
"they're happening according to the wish

00:09:16.295 --> 00:09:18.290
"of some divine force,

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:20.795
"and that divine force will
never think ill about you,

00:09:20.795 --> 00:09:24.280
"so you have to respect
that as being better."

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.289
So if things worked I said, "Fine."

00:09:27.289 --> 00:09:30.505
If they didn't, I felt
that there was some kind of

00:09:30.505 --> 00:09:33.329
divine force or divinity

00:09:33.329 --> 00:09:35.001
that didn't want me to do that

00:09:35.001 --> 00:09:38.201
and perhaps wait for another opportunity.

00:09:38.201 --> 00:09:40.081
The other thing he told me was

00:09:40.081 --> 00:09:44.248
when I was struggling a
lot in those times, I said,

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.682
(speaks in Hindi)

00:09:47.682 --> 00:09:51.625
So he said, (speaks in Hindi).

00:09:51.625 --> 00:09:54.822
I used to tell him life is a struggle.

00:09:54.822 --> 00:09:57.309
And he used to say, "So
long as there is life

00:09:57.309 --> 00:09:59.199
"there is going to be struggle."

00:09:59.199 --> 00:10:01.887
I think if you accept this factor

00:10:01.887 --> 00:10:04.507
that your life is never going to be easy,

00:10:04.507 --> 00:10:07.176
something or the other is going to happen,

00:10:07.176 --> 00:10:11.272
so rather than sit in some
kind of despondency on that

00:10:11.272 --> 00:10:14.246
and be aware that tomorrow
is another challenge,

00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:15.496
is another day,

00:10:17.503 --> 00:10:20.915
which could turn out to be
profitable, which may not,

00:10:20.915 --> 00:10:23.275
but eventually there will be another day

00:10:23.275 --> 00:10:25.616
when you can start trying all over again.

00:10:25.616 --> 00:10:27.416
- And then there was a lag in your career

00:10:27.416 --> 00:10:29.745
for almost 10 to 15 years,

00:10:29.745 --> 00:10:31.568
which is really the death of an actor,

00:10:31.568 --> 00:10:33.452
because you joined politics,

00:10:33.452 --> 00:10:34.852
then you made some bad decisions,

00:10:34.852 --> 00:10:36.500
and you didn't really sign films,

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.817
and then suddenly you
were a middle aged actor.

00:10:39.817 --> 00:10:43.423
What did you do to change
the rules of the game

00:10:43.423 --> 00:10:46.090
that today you are sitting here?

00:10:47.888 --> 00:10:49.560
- I think certain circumstances

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.218
are always at that point of time

00:10:51.218 --> 00:10:52.762
where I was given to understand

00:10:52.762 --> 00:10:55.721
that I didn't need to work anymore.

00:10:55.721 --> 00:10:59.842
And I took a sabbatical
for a couple of years,

00:10:59.842 --> 00:11:04.009
and now that I look back I
feel that that was a mistake

00:11:04.952 --> 00:11:05.964
and I shouldn't have done that,

00:11:05.964 --> 00:11:10.455
because a lot of water had
flown under the bridge so to say

00:11:10.455 --> 00:11:13.622
during the time that I wasn't working.

00:11:15.554 --> 00:11:16.975
It also teaches you

00:11:16.975 --> 00:11:20.024
that you're never going to be the best,

00:11:20.024 --> 00:11:23.854
you're never going to
be on top of the ladder.

00:11:23.854 --> 00:11:25.420
There will always be somebody

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.094
who's going to be younger, better than you

00:11:28.094 --> 00:11:29.430
who will take your place,

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.263
and this is quite common in sports

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:37.044
and in our profession as well.

00:11:37.044 --> 00:11:40.404
It takes a bit of time to understand that.

00:11:40.404 --> 00:11:43.909
I would be lying if I said that I

00:11:43.909 --> 00:11:46.174
was not comfortable with it.

00:11:46.174 --> 00:11:48.476
It was very discomforting,

00:11:48.476 --> 00:11:50.559
because you suddenly find

00:11:51.495 --> 00:11:55.272
that if you go into a public situation

00:11:55.272 --> 00:11:57.859
you find greater attention being given

00:11:57.859 --> 00:12:00.376
to the other person than yourself

00:12:00.376 --> 00:12:01.909
when all along your career

00:12:01.909 --> 00:12:04.058
you've been the center of attraction,

00:12:04.058 --> 00:12:06.277
and it takes a while to understand that.

00:12:06.277 --> 00:12:08.635
But I think that the
sooner you understand that

00:12:08.635 --> 00:12:10.620
the better for you as an actor.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:13.487
You're not going to be
able to be number one.

00:12:13.487 --> 00:12:15.037
You're never going to be able to

00:12:15.037 --> 00:12:19.204
attract the kind of commercials
or the public adulation,

00:12:20.825 --> 00:12:23.289
and you just want to carry on working

00:12:23.289 --> 00:12:25.279
because you enjoy the profession

00:12:25.279 --> 00:12:29.446
without all these other tributaries
that get attached to it.

00:12:30.566 --> 00:12:34.562
I look for challenges even
today, and even as I sit here

00:12:34.562 --> 00:12:38.263
I hope that tomorrow as I start a new film

00:12:38.263 --> 00:12:41.930
I will be able to look
at it as a challenge.

00:12:43.228 --> 00:12:46.499
I'm still very nervous when I go there.

00:12:46.499 --> 00:12:50.666
I feel that every new
opportunity that comes my way

00:12:51.521 --> 00:12:56.327
needs to be looked at as
that one door that will open,

00:12:56.327 --> 00:12:58.660
something which perhaps will

00:13:01.266 --> 00:13:04.120
stop me from being bankrupt again,

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.647
and stop me from getting
back into that situation

00:13:07.647 --> 00:13:11.230
where I had to plead
against all my debtors

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:15.289
to save me from this situation,

00:13:15.289 --> 00:13:18.358
and that's how I look at it every day.

00:13:18.358 --> 00:13:19.818
I hope that I can continue doing that,

00:13:19.818 --> 00:13:22.485
because I don't want this sword,

00:13:24.719 --> 00:13:27.730
this Damocles sword hanging on my head,

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:31.052
and therefore I continue
working so long as I can.

00:13:31.052 --> 00:13:33.249
But yes, in the back of my mind

00:13:33.249 --> 00:13:37.789
there will be those ugly
moments that plagued me,

00:13:37.789 --> 00:13:40.509
and the fear of them recurring again

00:13:40.509 --> 00:13:43.123
is perhaps what drives me every day.

00:13:43.123 --> 00:13:44.336
- You've said this in the past

00:13:44.336 --> 00:13:47.910
that you are really a director's actor,

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:48.956
that at the end of the day

00:13:48.956 --> 00:13:50.936
you don't have an idea of the film,

00:13:50.936 --> 00:13:54.676
but you absorb the director's
idea what he expects of you.

00:13:54.676 --> 00:13:56.454
- I think that it's important for you

00:13:56.454 --> 00:14:00.037
to leave the decision
making to one person.

00:14:01.697 --> 00:14:04.073
I like to listen to one person on set,

00:14:04.073 --> 00:14:05.681
and if he's the captain of the ship

00:14:05.681 --> 00:14:07.197
I will do what he asks me to do,

00:14:07.197 --> 00:14:09.212
because he has the visual,

00:14:09.212 --> 00:14:12.059
and the sense, and the idea in his mind

00:14:12.059 --> 00:14:14.180
of what he wants to make.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.872
If you have the disagreement with him,

00:14:15.872 --> 00:14:17.762
have the disagreement during the time

00:14:17.762 --> 00:14:20.024
when he's narrating the script to you.

00:14:20.024 --> 00:14:22.161
Have all your arguments and
discussions before that,

00:14:22.161 --> 00:14:24.014
but once you come on set

00:14:24.014 --> 00:14:28.012
I think it's only very
professional and ethical

00:14:28.012 --> 00:14:30.493
to follow what the
director tells you to do.

00:14:30.493 --> 00:14:33.326
- If this 71 year old Mr. Bachchan

00:14:34.263 --> 00:14:36.436
could go back to the younger Amitabh

00:14:36.436 --> 00:14:38.311
and hold his hand and say,

00:14:38.311 --> 00:14:41.829
"Don't take this path, go here instead,"

00:14:41.829 --> 00:14:43.605
at what moment would that be?

00:14:43.605 --> 00:14:45.355
- I'd never say that,

00:14:46.401 --> 00:14:49.568
because I think that whatever happened

00:14:51.784 --> 00:14:53.086
I wouldn't want to change it.

00:14:53.086 --> 00:14:57.141
I feel that many of the
mishaps, or the wrong decisions,

00:14:57.141 --> 00:14:59.308
or the failures that I had

00:15:01.105 --> 00:15:03.634
were great learning grounds for me.

00:15:03.634 --> 00:15:05.967
I think I'm a lot wiser now.

00:15:06.964 --> 00:15:08.518
No one is the ultimate person

00:15:08.518 --> 00:15:10.931
as far as wisdom is concerned,

00:15:10.931 --> 00:15:15.014
so I feel that I wouldn't
want to change anything

00:15:16.334 --> 00:15:18.556
and just continue the way I'm going.

00:15:18.556 --> 00:15:19.853
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:19.853 --> 00:15:22.962
I made this video because
Faks Jilan asked me to.

00:15:22.962 --> 00:15:24.084
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:15:24.084 --> 00:15:25.907
that you want me to profile next,

00:15:25.907 --> 00:15:27.078
leave it down in the comments below

00:15:27.078 --> 00:15:28.141
and I'll see what I can do.

00:15:28.141 --> 00:15:30.576
I'd also love to know what
did you learn from this video

00:15:30.576 --> 00:15:32.030
that really resonated with you?

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:33.982
What point did you take home the most?

00:15:33.982 --> 00:15:35.331
What changes are you going to make

00:15:35.331 --> 00:15:38.026
in your life or your business
after watching this video?

00:15:38.026 --> 00:15:39.521
Please leave in the comments below,

00:15:39.521 --> 00:15:41.263
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:15:41.263 --> 00:15:42.480
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:43.752
to Katherine Carini.

00:15:43.752 --> 00:15:44.787
Katherine, thank you so much

00:15:44.787 --> 00:15:46.213
for picking up a copy of my book.

00:15:46.213 --> 00:15:48.276
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:15:48.276 --> 00:15:49.948
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:15:49.948 --> 00:15:51.300
I believe in you.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.816
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:15:53.816 --> 00:15:56.913
and whatever your one
word is, much love guys.

00:15:56.913 --> 00:15:57.997
I'll see you soon.

00:15:57.997 --> 00:16:00.080
(swoosh)

